<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<gpx version="1.1" creator="Good Shit" xmlns="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1"><trk><name>Affensteine: Wilde Hölle, Carolafelsen and Idagrotte Loop — Whole original Nasser Grund–Eulentilke–Carolafelsen–Idagrotte loop [planning only — not navigation-grade]</name><desc>The original Affensteine loop near Bad Schandau is unavailable because fallen trees and further tree-break danger block its required Eulentilke gully. Attempt it only after specific official reopening; no date is established. Jordanweg is a different route. If reopened, start at Nasser Grund parking, climb Eulentilke and Wilde Hölle, take the return spur to Carolafelsen, and continue via Reitsteig to the exposed Idagrotte ledge and cave-tunnel return spur. Descend via Königsweg, Hinterer Heideweg and Dietrichsgrund to the public Beuthenfall junction, then return on Flößersteig and Nasser Grund. The fenced waterfall and climbing accesses are excluded. The roughly 7.7 km expert loop requires sure-footedness, confidence with exposure, grippy footwear and junction preparation. Choose dry, ice-free daylight; avoid wet rock, high winds and poor visibility. Markings are incomplete. Stay on existing paths and obey current restrictions. Any unavailable required leg closes the whole itinerary. Approximately 420 m ascent and descent and four hours remain unmeasured planning estimates; the map is for planning rather than turn-by-turn navigation.

Route geometry: © OpenStreetMap contributors, Open Database License (ODbL) 1.0 — https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright; https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/1-0/. Source: https://api.openstreetmap.org/api/0.6/map?bbox=14.217,50.907,14.253,50.930. Changes: 69 directed original-node intervals from 52 whole source ways, retaining both exact viewpoint/cave returns and removing only duplicate shared junction nodes. All 424 selected positions are literal source vertices; canonical display uses the existing source-vertex simplifier. No snapping, synthetic connectors, manager/publisher coordinates or elevations. Two-dimensional planning geometry, not navigation-grade, current walkability or legal permission.

